Date: Wed, 8 Sep 93 19:12:07 PDT
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Cypherpunks: political or what?

Nick Szabo writes:

> Tim May:
> > The Cypherpunks group is most definitely not a political action group!
> 
> Nonsense!  Cypherpunks spend a quite a lot of time discussing
> and even taking action on political concerns -- Clipper, BBS

By "political action group" I meant a group organizing to nominate
candidates, to win elections, propose legislation, etc. I regret that
I tried to be pithy here, instead of my usual long-winded self.

Some in Cypherpunks want more of a focus on privacy issues, but others
want the focus on crypto methods and tools. Why? There are already
several lobbying groups for privacy, electronic civil rights, etc.
For example:

-the Electronic Frontier Foundation (EFF) has money, lawyers, offices,
newsletters, etc. Lawsuits, etc. 

-the American Civil Liberties Union (ACLU) has several branches
working on legislations, lawsuits, etc.

-the Computer Professionals for Social Responsibility (CPSR) is
similar to the above groups.

There are other groups, too. But none of them has the focus the
Cypherpunks group has, which means we occupy an important niche that
they can't fill.

Also, many Cypherpunks react violently to be labelled as
"libertarians" (and I don't mean people like me...I mean people who
really claim to be "anarcho-syndicalists" or even Maoists! I won't
argue their views here.). So we are certainly not a conventional
political action group...our main ideological commonality is the
belief that strong crypto is important.

> Potentially, cypherpunks could be more business-oriented than
> political.  Cypherpunks don't, I'm afraid, have a very
> good grasp of business -- there is a definite lack of people
> experienced in business willing to talk about cypherpunks business
> opportunity.  A telling point -- the major public cypherpunks-related
> legal talent is wrapped up in a purely political activity (suing
> the NSA over FOIA), rather than in unravelling the sticky legal
> webs surrounding digital cash and offshore banking (perhaps the
> most important legal problems of crypto-anarchy). 

Perhaps this is so, but this is how he currently wishes to spend his
money, of which he has a whole lot (John Gilmore was employee #5 at
Sun, retired in the mid-80s, then put $15K into helping fund Cygnus
Support...which is now a rapidly expanding company). John's interest
in suing the NSA does not match _my_ current interests, but it is what
he thinks is important....and it could turn out to have some important
implications.

> It turns out there is a lot of under-wraps "trade secret" business 
> planning, but much of what surfaces turns out to be fairly naive and not 
> worthy of having been kept a trade secret.  The businesses would sprout 
> much faster and be far more effective if at least their broad outlines
> were publically discussed.  Also, public discussion of the vast markets 
> would motivate more people to hurdle the learning curve (obscure math, 
> mail software, legal issues, etc.).

I agree with Nick's comments, but doubt that the businesses would
sprout much faster...so far we haven't really hit on any obvious
business opportunities, regardless of the secrecy sometimes in
evidence. (In other words, the once secret ideas of six months ago,
now revealed, are not leading to any real businesses...so the secrecy
was not a critical delaying factor.)

> I also disagree that crypto should not be "pushed."  Another
> important cypherpunks activity is cracking weak crypto and pointing
> out (often by illustration) how people's privacy is or can be

Except I can't think of many cases (any cases?) when we did this kind
of cryptanalysis or traffic analysis, and I've been involved since
about 90 Days B.C. (Before Cypherpunks). It _could_ be an important
activity, and I hope Nick makes this one of his special interests.

> etc.  Much of crypto-anarchy relies on a "fax effect", where
> the value of PGP, anon remailers, digicash, etc. are proportional
> to the number of people using them.  "Spreading the meme"
> is thus a critical cypherpunk activity, just as it is in
> electoral politics.

I agree here, and I would recommend Steven Levy's article on "Crypto
Rebels" in "Wired," Issue 1.2, and Kevin Kelly's piece on "The
Technologies of Disconnection" in "Whole Earth Review," Summer, 1993.


