Date: Wed, 8 Sep 93 19:13:10 PDT
From: fschulz@pyramid.com
Subject: Tech: chord keyboards

I am designing a chordic keyboard because
	1) conventional keyboards hurt my hands
	2) I want to type faster
	3) conventional keyboards are too bulky

I checked out all the keyboards that exist and found none I liked.
This was very disapointing, as I really wanted to buy a keyboard
I found comfortable.

Keyboard characteristics I want:
	1) Low key depression force
	2) Minimal finger movement
	3) Keyboard fits in pocket

1) and 2) contribute to speed and reduce hand stress.
I want typing to be an integrated part of my activities.
For example, I want to be able to eat an apple as I type.
This requires a one-handed input device and 3).

I want this keyboard to be the only keyboard I use.
Thus the electrical interface will be IBM PC compatible.
This is very useful as most X-terminals are IBM PS2 compatible.
The X-terminal I use at work is PS2 compatible, so I would
be able to carry this keyboard around and use it everywhere.

The keyboard would have an onboard programmable, nonvolatile
memory to store chord to keycode mappings. The mapping will
be fully programmable by the user. I have memorized several
chord mappings and I refuse to learn any more. I consider
a user programmable mapping to be an absolute requirement.
The BAT is not programmable and their built in mapping is
pretty stupid.

I now know more about keyboards than I ever wanted to know.

Designing the cpu card and microcode is much easier than I
expected. Finding the mechanical key switches, which I thought
would be easy, is virtually impossible. I have been looking
for 6 months with no success. I now have lots of unusable samples.
Getting low activation force is the problem.
