I said recently that I had reposted my essay "Libertaria in Cyberspace" to
this list. Apparently I misspoke, as I can find no evidence for this.
Instead, I apparently had only reposted it to the _Cypherpunks_ list.

So in the interests of explaining more about what I've been talking about
lately, and because several people have asked for an e-mail copy, here it
is.

Bear in mind that it's a year old now, and a few things have changed.
Implementation details are mostly left out, though I think a lot of
progress has been made in the past year, especially via the Cypherpunks
activities. A lot more work is needed, which should surprise nobody.

When I posted this the first time, a nice debate ensued, with Perry Metzger
arguing that the notion of "moving into cyberspace" is misleading. I would
say more about Perry's position, but I don't want to misrepresent his views
and his objections to this scheme. Duncan Frissell also had some very good
points at the time, about ATMs, international banking, and the way
cyberspace will basically evolve in the anarchic, uncontrolled way the Net
is currently evolving. Again, I want to avoid putting words in their mouth.
Perhaps, if there's interest, they can repost their comments.



