From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: Crypto-anarchy

>>[strong crypto ban]

Martin Olah:
>Considering that encoded radio transmissions are already illegal, I see this 
>as a very strong possibility. Of course it may be a lot harder to track down
>email than it is to locate a radio transmitter, but I would prepare now for 
>this eventuality. 

Several points to be made here:

* You mean "encryption" of radio, not "encoding".  That ban,
dating from WWI is based, like the Fairness Doctrine and other
radio crap on "public ownership of the airwaves".  The encryption
ban is now under pressure from both the cellular phone industry 
and the increasing numbers of independent, international hams.  Comsat 
broadcast encryption (eg HBO scrambling, data over data and phone 
lines) is already legal.

* Strong software crypto, esp. PGP, is spread across 100,000's of 
computers and dozens of countries.  Ironically, the Clipper
initiative raised awareness and accelerated the spread of
PGP.  DES is used by thousands of banks in over a hundred countries.  
Large encrypted files can be hidden in normal image or music data
(steganography).  Traffic analysis of e-mail can be foiled by "digital 
mixes", which are anonymous remailers as currently implemented plus 
random-time delays and other features.  A ban would make life
harder, but plenty of people worldwide would still find it worth
their while to use crypto without U.S. trapdoors.

This doesn't mean cypherpunks don't have our hands full with things
to do.  PGP is too awkward for routine use, but this can be fixed
with good GUIs and integration with popular mailers.  Our cypherpunks
remailers need to be enhanced to become more like "digital mixes", 
resistant to easy traffic analysis.  We need a bullet-proof protocol
for digital cash to implement in software.  We need to get the
independent BBS industry on board (already >$1 billion, much of
it in legally ambiguous territory -- porn, software "piracy", in
many cases involving unsecure communication of credit card numbers,
monitoring of True Name customers, etc.)  We need to figure out the 
legal and business ramifications of digital cash, various business
proposals, integration with the current banking system, etc.

I invite all those interested in undertaking this history-making
pioneer work to subscribe to cypherpunks@toad.com, and if in the
Bay Area attend our meeting on Saturday.  (Other regions, eg
Boston and London, also have meetings which are often announced
on the list).

>I would eventually like the mass media attack, such as an Extropian
>rock band, since I'm getting very tired of mindless celebrities ruling the
>airwaves. Popular culture needs a major kick in the ass.

If you're a script writer or director or pop star, this is a good way 
to be effective.  If you are hacker (or mathematician or lawyer or 
businessman), crypto-anarchy is a great way to make a big difference.
In general, political activism can take a wide variety of forms
vastly more effective than punching cards in curtained booths.

